{"t":1789589215,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"d827b9f3-5460-4c11-9584-3ab27f5e429b","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-09-16T16:49:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 161649\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\nIssued by National Weather Service State College PA\n1249 PM EDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nThe forecast remains on track, so no major adjustments were\nnecessary. The primary period of uncertainty continues to be\nSunday into early next week, as a frontal system meanders \nnearby. This is reflected in a highly uncertain temperature \nforecast.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Turning warmer and more humid with a return of shower and\n  thunderstorm chances for Thursday and Friday.\n\n- 2) Rain-free and less muggy conditions are likely on Saturday,\n  before the weather turns more unsettled again from Sunday into\n  next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Turning warmer and more humid with a return of shower and\nthunderstorm chances for Thursday and Friday.\n\nA ridge of high pressure, both at the surface and aloft, is\ncontinuing to provide sunny skies this afternoon, with\ntemperatures heading well into the 80s for most areas. This \nwill lead into a mostly clear start to the night, but cloudiness\nis expected to increase after midnight from the north and west \n(first encroaching on the Alleghenies).\n\nFor Thursday, the prospects for showers and thunderstorms will\nincrease by mid to late afternoon, especially for the \nAlleghenies, the Shenandoah Valley, and the metroplex. This is\ntied to a slow moving cold front, making a gradual approach from\nthe Ohio Valley and Pennsylvania. As you head farther southeast\nthrough Virginia and into the coastal plain, the chances for\nrain should decrease. Thursday looks like the warmest day of the\nweek, although this is contingent on how much sunshine/heating\ncan take place earlier in the day. At this juncture, we're\nexpecting highs well into the 80s again, with parts of the\nShenandoah Valley up towards the metroplex perhaps making a run\nat 90.\n\nBy Friday, the frontal boundary itself should be across central\nto southern VA. This will lead to a continued chance of showers,\nalong with somewhat cooler readings as compared to Thursday.  \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain-free and less muggy conditions are likely on Saturday,\nbefore the weather turns more unsettled again from Sunday into \nnext week.\n\nCurrent indications point to a rain-free start to the weekend,\nwith the above mentioned cold front pushing down into the\nCarolinas, and weak high pressure building down from the north.\n\nHowever, signals also point to more unsettled weather from\nSunday into next week, as a persistent upper ridge axis gets \nshunted towards the deep South, allowing upper-level impulses \nto move across the Mid-Atlantic from the west. This should\nprovide the impetus for at least hit and miss showers and\nthunderstorms on a daily basis.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /16Z WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\n15z TAF update for DCA, IAD and BWI features only very minor \nchanges. Predominantly VFR conds are expected areawide through \n18z Thurs. Expect a light southerly wind of 5-10 kts this \nafternoon and generally light and variable winds tonight. A \nlight southerly breeze will develop again on Thursday.\n\nPrevious discussion below....\n\nWhile Canadian high pressure has slowly moved toward the\nAtlantic coast, its influence will help maintain VFR conditions\ninto Thursday morning. Gradients remain weak with light \nsoutherly winds expected. Ahead of an approaching cold front, \nshowers and thunderstorms return to the forecast on Thursday. \nThe western TAF sites would see the most likely impacts, with \nconvection likely to weaken further to the east. Outside of any \nstorm development, VFR conditions continue on Thursday with \nsoutherly winds around 5 to 10 knots. A cold front moves through\nthe region early Friday which yields a shift to northerly \nwinds. Afternoon gusts could approach 15 to 20 knots, with any \nstorm chances back toward central Virginia.\n\nHigh pressure passing to our north shifts winds out of the east to \nsoutheast Saturday, turning southwesterly by Sunday. Over the \nweekend, gusts up to 15 to perhaps 20 knots are possible. In \ngeneral, expect predominantly VFR conditions to persist, but \nflight conditions could deteriorate as shower and thunderstorm \npotential increase late in the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nEarlier elevated southerly winds have weakened with maximum \ngusts currently around 10 to 15 knots. Canadian high pressure \nremains nearby into tonight which will favor tranquil weather \nover the waterways.\n\nBy Thursday, this ridge edges offshore ahead of the next cold \nfront. Ahead of this system, southerly winds increase which \ncould near Small Craft Advisory levels on Thursday \nafternoon/evening. This is particularly the case over wider \nwaters of the Chesapeake Bay due to southerly channeling. The \ncold front crosses the waters early Friday which shifts winds \nover to northerly. Breezy conditions in the wake could again \nnear advisory levels. As high pressure tracks across southern \nQuebec, marine winds turn northeasterly into Friday night with \ngusts around 10 to 15 knots.\n\nHigh pressure passing to our north shifts winds out of the east to \nsoutheast Saturday, turning southwesterly on Sunday. Over the \nweekend, gusts up to 15 to perhaps 20 knots are possible.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nAs winds have turned more south to southeasterly, the onshore\nflow has raised tidal anomalies to just below 1 foot. This has\nbeen enough to push a few of the more sensitive locations into \nthe Action stage. Until the cold front moves through early \nFriday, water levels will remain somewhat elevated. Although no\ntidal site is shown to experience any coastal flooding, it will\nneed to be monitored. This is particularly the case at \nAnnapolis, especially during the higher of the two astronomical\nhigh tides.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...99\nAVIATION...BRO/KVH/99\nMARINE...BRO/KVH\n"},"forecast":{"t":1789589184,"days":{"2026-09-16":{"hi":87,"lo":67,"pop":3,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-09-17":{"hi":90,"lo":72,"pop":49,"short":"Mostly Sunny then Slight Chance Showers And Thunderstorms"},"2026-09-18":{"hi":86,"lo":64,"pop":7,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-09-19":{"hi":83,"lo":66,"pop":7,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-09-20":{"hi":86,"lo":67,"pop":62,"short":"Chance Showers And Thunderstorms"},"2026-09-21":{"hi":78,"lo":65,"pop":44,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-22":{"hi":76,"lo":63,"pop":45,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"}}},"grid":{"t0":1789588800,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,11700,10000,10000,10000,10000,10000,10000,25000,25000,25000,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0],"spd":[8,8,7,6,5,4,4,3,3,2,3,3,3,3,3,3,4,4,5,6,7,7,8,8,9,9,8,8,6,5,4,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,4,5,6,8,9,9,9,8,8],"gst":[12,12,11,11,10,9,9,8,8,7,7,7,7,7,8,8,8,8,9,9,10,11,11,12,13,13,13,12,11,10,9,8,8,7,7,7,7,7,7,8,9,10,12,13,14,13,13,13],"dir":[160,170,170,170,170,180,180,180,190,190,200,190,190,190,190,200,190,190,190,180,180,180,170,170,170,170,180,170,170,200,190,200,210,230,240,270,280,290,300,330,340,350,0,0,0,10,10,10],"pop":[1,1,1,1,0,1,2,3,3,3,1,1,1,0,2,3,6,8,8,8,10,10,11,12,12,12,12,11,27,33,41,41,20,3,6,6,6,6,6,6,1,1,1,1,1,1,4,4],"temp":[27.2,27.8,26.7,25.6,2